atement saying the  storming of the capital was trump's  doing  and that he did nothing to stop the  violence  she said there has never been a greater  betrayal by a president  of the united states of his office and  his oath to the constitution  cheney's move lays bare a split among  republicans  over trump and the future of their party  i'm joined now by dw reporter peter  rollerdale peter  impeachment is moving forward at a swift  pace in the house of representatives  what's the latest on the efforts to  remove trump from office  well today really is set to be a  historic day donald trump all for sure  to become  the first president in u.s history to be  impeached twice  it is really notable that over a dozen  republicans have indicated that they'll  back that effort  just over a year ago the first time  donald trump was impeached  exactly zero house republicans voted to  impeach  but the insurrection on january 6 really  does appear to have been the last role  for many in his party high-ranking  republican  member uh liz cheney released a strongly  worded statement saying that  there's never been a greater betrayal by  a president of the united states  of his office it's owed to the  constitution it really does signal a  strong break  with trump who's fighting himself  increasingly isolated athletes the white  house  and that many republicans really see the  party at a crossroads you know according  reports  senate majority-led uh senate majority  leader mitch mcconnell  has even indicated that uh he believes  impeaching  trump is not just the easiest way to get  rid of the president but also get rid of  trumpism from the republican party  whether that translates into trump  actually being removed from office is a  different question  you would still need a two-thirds  majority uh in the senate to do that  and that's all but guaranteed yeah he's  a man under siege what's the president  himself been saying about all of this  he's been  digging his heels in somewh